2832805393112416 and 0.42913823911630933 both had correct=false; effort, feasibility, and verified-gap aggregates were unchanged, with slightly different control_pass_rate. Incumbent combined_score=0.53012102014295, correct=true. Failure cause unknown. Alternatives were additional caching, repeated prefix certification, costly exhaustive large-panel search, and unsupported Borda/training/paper operations. Selected a minimax hinge envelope coupling retained ranks through one support budget; its practical benefit is untested. No program/evaluator execution, computational probes, package installation, or literature search occurred. Canonical local directory finalized once after ID/title: ideas/03_coupled_clipped_score_certificates_for_targeted_investment/memory.md; no alternate same-cycle target or staging memory was created or used.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T13:18:02.192017+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.42832805393112416, correct=False; scientific acceptance failed; cause unavailable; correct=false alone does not establish a protocol or implementation defect; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.42832805393112416, \"control_pass_rate\": 0.9525462962962963, \"correct\": false, \"feasible_rate\": 0.4027777777777778, \"mean_effort_fraction\": 0.7963369070825209, \"mean_verified_gap_fraction\": 0.18981481481481483}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment in Multitask Leaderboards\",\n  \"central_question\": \"Under a bounded, target-only score-response model with fixed rivals, how many task coordinates must be improved to attain strict first place, and how does that investment depend on the aggregation rule and response ceiling?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"In the evaluated public score panels, aggregation robustness is conditional on the response ceiling: exact six-task results reverse the mean–median minimum-support ordering between half and full headroom on matched attainable targets, while larger subject-level panels establish feasible investment bounds rather than exact rule rankings.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"This is a bounded counterfactual empirical study, not a new optimizer or training study. Existing final decision records support a direct rule-by-response comparison, including an exact matched-cohort reversal on the six-task Open LLM panel and explicit uncertainty about optimal support on the 57-subject HELM panel. The same scientific question organizes these as complementary evidence blocks with different certificate strength, not as transfer or discovery-order experiments. The frozen reference procedure is the studied method; unsuccessful search proposals are not the contribution. No universal ordering, practical training cost, or causal contamination effect is inferred.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"No edit context, previous_paper directory, prior manuscript, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is staged. The rejected status denotes rejection of an assumed inherited improvement story, not rejection of an actual manuscript. Compare three alternatives explicitly: preserve an optimizer-improvement story, broaden to a shared novel certificate family, or recenter on response-dependent investment comparisons. Choose the third: no search improvement was selected, component ablations are absent, and exact rule contrasts are stronger than claims of algorithmic novelty.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"Directly inspected source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py and the baseline counterpart; their source files are identical. The selected procedure exhausts panels with at most six coordinates, uses sorted headroom for larger mean panels, and marginal-score greedy supports for larger median and trimmed-mean panels.\",\n    \"Read final_evaluation independently of downstream_study. Its completed, qualified evaluation supports a finished empirical study; it does not establish population generalization or solver novelty.\",\n    \"The ordinary primary six-task records contain exact support outcomes, whereas the 57-subject median and trimmed-mean records retain bounded support outcomes. This changes the center of gravity from solver improvement to aggregation sensitivity with explicit certificate scope.\",\n    \"The separate auxiliary BBH and six-coordinate projections add sensitivity and verification depth, not a new architecture, new model training condition, or a pooled primary benchmark.\",\n    \"The local ICLR style is single-column, with a 5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height. This supports full-text-width, low-density comparisons rather than two-column dashboard figures.\",\n    \"The supplied bibliography contains metadata retrieval records, many tangential to this study, without evidence of substantive reading. Its presence does not license scientific positioning or citation padding. No external source was accessed during strategy generation.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Frozen bounded-headroom support-minimization procedure\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the selected studied procedure, not a newly successful proposal: improve selected coordinates by alpha times their remaining headroom, hold rivals fixed, and report exact support, certified infeasibility, or an honest lower–upper support interval. Explain exhaustive small-panel search, exact mean sorting, and larger-panel greedy witnesses separately.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Equal-task arithmetic mean\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Provides the directly grounded aggregation comparator and an analytically exact sorted-headroom solution; retain it in every main rule comparison.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Conventional median\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The central exact six-task contrast uses the conventional median, including the average of the two middle values for an even task count; distinguish this from a lower or upper median.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Symmetric 20-percent trimmed mean\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Provides the grounded intermediate order-statistic condition: remove floor(m/5) values from each tail. It checks whether the evidence is specific to the median without extending to untested ordinal rules.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"No-intervention, seeded-random, and marginal-score-greedy investment policies\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"These comparators already have matched final decision records. They contextualize support selection, retain zero and negative differences, and show that the selected procedure coincides with greedy in important regimes. They are not component ablations or independent model-training methods.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Primary thesis test: ordinary Open LLM primary decisions only; match by seed and target slot and retain targets attainable under both mean and median at alpha=0.5 and alpha=1.0. Compare exact support fractions on that identical per-seed cohort across both response conditions. The recorded data give a negative median-minus-mean average at half headroom and a positive average at full headroom for every final seed. This is a descriptive within-snapshot reversal, not a pre-registered significance claim.\",\n    \"Report attainability for all sampled ordinary primary targets at alpha=0.25, 0.5, and 1.0, separately for HELM and Open LLM, so conditioning the exact contrast on attainable cases cannot conceal infeasible targets.\",\n    \"Report support cost with exactness attached: exact support for the six-task panel and the mean, but evaluator lower bounds and selected feasible upper bounds for larger median/trimmed panels. A reversal of greedy witness costs on HELM must not be called a reversal of minimum costs when bound intervals overlap.\",\n    \"Retain matched policy comparisons, preferably a compact main table at alpha=0.5 and the complete grid after references. State where exact selection reduces support relative to greedy and where it ties. Existing paired intervals compare the selected procedure against random or greedy; they are not cross-rule confidence intervals.\",\n    \"Keep constant-ceiling, zero-response, permutation, and replay checks separate from ordinary performance. A passed check establishes only the property tested, not universal scientific validity or optimality.\",\n    \"Use auxiliary six-coordinate projections and revision-matched BBH decisions only as separately labeled sensitivity evidence. Do not merge them with primary scores, compare their target slots as identical model identities, or claim domain transfer.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early overview: fixed public task scores and rivals; target-only bounded-headroom intervention; three aggregation rules leading to exact support, infeasibility, or an interval. No fabricated score matrix or illustrative numerical result.\",\n    \"Figure 2, central exact comparison: two matched-cohort dot plots, one for half headroom and one for full headroom, showing median-minus-mean minimum-support fraction by final sampling seed with a zero reference line.\",\n    \"Figure 3, scope and cost: a 2-by-2 composite with one row per primary panel family, all-target attainability at three response ceilings on the left, and paired attainable-cohort support bounds at half/full headroom on the right. Exact values and unresolved intervals use visibly different marks.\",\n    \"Use tables, not additional main figures, for snapshot scope, comparator differences, and certificate/status counts. Put the full conditional comparator-interval grid and auxiliary sensitivities after references.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"investment_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the counterfactual investment object, fixed-rival assumption, aggregation conditions, and certificate outcomes legible before quantitative results.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"score intervention and certificate scope\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; qualitative specification only\",\n      \"split_by\": \"fixed scores, bounded intervention, aggregation and support outcome\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"In the inspected single-column ICLR style, full width is 5.5 inches, not a two-column spread. Use a shallow approximately 5.5-by-1.8-inch diagram on page 1 or 2; a half-width 2.75-inch rendering would compress the update equation and outcome labels. Keep at most six visual groups and three aggregator branches, with at least 9-point rendered labels.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"content_constraints\": \"Show x_j changing to x_j+alpha(1-x_j) only on the selected support and label rivals fixed. Three aggregators are conditions, not sequential improvements. Distinguish exact, infeasible, and bounded outcomes. Do not draw training, an architecture transition, a novel certificate mechanism, or numerical toy measurements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"exact_rule_contrast\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Display the exact within-snapshot mean–median ordering reversal without infeasibility penalties, composition changes across response conditions, or search chronology.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"final sampling seed\",\n      \"metric\": \"mean exact median-minus-mean support fraction on a fixed jointly attainable cohort\",\n      \"split_by\": \"alpha=0.5 versus alpha=1.0 on the ordinary Open LLM primary panel\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Two side-by-side panels occupy approximately 5.5 by 2.1 inches in the 5.5-inch single text column. Each panel has three seed categories and one contrast series, so 9-point labels and a common signed y scale remain readable; include a horizontal zero line and cohort counts in the caption. Do not connect response conditions as a time trajectory.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1,\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"content_constraints\": \"Filter ordinary primary Open LLM decisions, join rule/alpha cells by seed and slot, and intersect attainability under both rules at both response conditions. Each seed retains the same cohort in both panels. Plot exact support-count differences divided by six; show individual seed summaries, not invented confidence intervals or a pooled population p-value.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"attainability_and_support_scope\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show which targets can attain first place and how much support is established, while exposing differences between exact and bounded evidence.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"aggregation rule\",\n      \"metric\": \"left: target attainment rate; right: normalized support lower and feasible upper bounds\",\n      \"split_by\": \"HELM 57-subject panel versus Open LLM six-task panel\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"A 2-by-2 figure at approximately 5.5 by 3.4 inches uses the full single-column width. Two panels per row, three ceiling categories at most, three rule series, shared scales, and a common legend avoid shrinking a dashboard. Use at least 9-point final labels and a short caption explicitly defining the support cohorts and interval semantics.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 4,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"content_constraints\": \"Left panels include every ordinary primary target at all three recorded ceilings and use one attainment definition: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim. Right panels use only alpha=0.5 and 1.0, intersect attainable targets across all three rules within each seed and ceiling, and report the cohort sizes. Average the within-seed lower and upper support fractions with declared equal seed weights; the interval is an identification/certification range, not a confidence interval. Cohorts here may differ across ceilings, unlike Figure 2. Exact costs collapse to points. Never draw the infeasible sentinel m+1 as an actual cost. If a required joint cohort is empty, show not estimable rather than imputation.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"supplementary_plan\": {\n    \"purpose\": \"Provide substantive mathematical, conditional-uncertainty, sensitivity, and reproducibility depth after references without hiding the central exact contrast, unattainability, or larger-panel certification limits from the main body.\",\n    \"planned_subsections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Support objective, aggregation conventions, and certificates\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Give the complete target-only objective, strict-first tolerance, even median and trimming conventions, mean sorted-headroom argument, exhaustive six-coordinate procedure, full-uplift infeasibility test, and the conservative lower-bound versus feasible-witness distinction. Tie explanations to the inspected frozen source and task/evaluate_final.py; do not describe abandoned bounds as evaluated contributions.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Complete rule-by-response and comparator tables\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Tabulate ordinary primary decisions by family, seed, rule, and ceiling, retaining attainment counts, exact/interval status, lower bounds, feasible support sizes, and all zero/random/greedy references. Include the already-recorded per-seed cluster-percentile intervals for selected-minus-random and selected-minus-greedy effort, their conditional scope and all null effects; do not reinterpret them as cross-rule inference.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.feasible_rate\", \"final.metric.mean_effort_fraction\", \"final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Task granularity and auxiliary sensitivity\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Separate auxiliary six-task projections from the BBH subtask subset using family, panel, and slot routing in task/evaluate_final.py. Report the already-recorded auxiliary outcomes, revision reconciliation, and distinct evaluation-setting caveat. No new resampling experiment, unrun ablation, or causal granularity attribution is promised.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Matched controls and evaluation scope\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Describe constant-ceiling, zero-response, column-permutation and reset-replay checks, including replay statistical weight zero. Report the completed final qualification faithfully and explain that it does not prove every minimum-support statement or independent benchmark generalization. Retain the evidence trust limitation rather than promoting candidate diagnostics into verified facts.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.control_pass_rate\", \"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Frozen selection and unsuccessful alternatives\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Provide a concise nonchronological disclosure of baseline selection, zero search-score change, three scored alternatives, and zero unscored alternatives. None was a qualifying search winner. Report unsuccessful outcomes without inventing a defect, adaptivity explanation, or measured loss for absent evidence; keep search quantities separate from final measurements.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"study.investigation_outcome\", \"study.search_score_delta\", \"study.attempts.total\", \"study.attempts.scored\", \"study.attempts.unscored\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Public data provenance and reproduction map\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Use existing source records and manifest to document HELM release, the two pinned Open LLM revisions, acquisition time, source and extracted-record hashes, normalization, alias grouping, filtering, target strata, seeds, dependency versions, and CPU commands. Map every eventual plot/table to its derived-data script and source slice; mark those scripts as a writer-stage deliverable, not already present. State unresolved score-data redistribution licensing and variable evaluation settings.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the documented LLM role in research ideation, code preparation, reference-query planning, and manuscript framing; add writing/figure assistance only when actually used. State that counterfactual score edits are not model training. Do not invent model identities, provider details, human validation, or a literature-exposure history.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"study.attempts.total\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"forbidden_headings\": [\n    \"Search Progression\", \"Campaign Phases\", \"Milestones\", \"First Valid Result\", \"Timeline\", \"Experiment A\", \"Experiment B\", \"From Baseline to Winner\", \"Optimization Breakthroughs\", \"Successful Certificate Variants\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_phrases\": [\n    \"we then tried\", \"first valid result\", \"subsequent improvements\", \"next-best\", \"later variants\", \"winning implementation\", \"state of the art\", \"universally most robust aggregation\", \"median always requires more investment\", \"measured training cost\", \"causal contamination effect\", \"all full-panel solutions are optimal\", \"correctness rate\", \"correct and eligible\", \"eligible\", \"combined_score\", \"rubric\", \"worker_01\", \"worker_02\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_writer\": [\n    \"Format contract: target the provided ICLR 2026 single-column style and anonymized review format. The inspected paper.sty declares textwidth 5.5in and textheight 9in. Here preferred_span=full_width means the one full text column; a column-width two-column convention does not apply. Do not replace the venue with the NeurIPS template mentioned in source-task provenance.\",\n    \"Body plan: aim for about 8.5 substantive pages, excluding references: 1.25 pages for abstract/introduction and contribution scope, 0.75 for safely grounded related work and modeling context, 1.0 for objective/assumptions, 1.0 for solver and certificate scope, 0.75 for experimental protocol and uncertainty, 2.75 for exact contrast/attainability/support/comparators including figures, and 1.0 for limitations and conclusion. Figure area is included, not added on top. An eight-page body must fill at least 75 percent of its last page; never pad to reach this threshold.\",\n    \"Suggested thesis-first headings: Introduction; Related Work and Scope; Bounded-Response Investment; Computing Support and Certifying Limits; Public Score Panels and Comparison Protocol; Response-Dependent Aggregation Costs; Attainability and Certificate Scope; Limitations and Conclusion. No campaign-order sections.\",\n    \"Define x in [0,1], selected support S, target update x_j+alpha(1-x_j) for j in S, fixed competitors, and unique first as aggregate(target)>max rival aggregate+1e-10. Investment is |S|, normalized by panel task count for cross-panel display. Total normalized score lift is secondary and is not training examples, compute, dollars, or contamination severity. Alpha is a stipulated response fraction, not an estimated learning curve.\",\n    \"State equal task weighting, no spillover or regression on untouched coordinates, fixed rival pool, and score normalization explicitly. A support-cardinality comparison across six tasks and 57 subjects does not equate their real-world intervention costs. Avoid interpreting named architectures or task metadata as controlled architecture experiments.\",\n    \"Private-only grounding audit: final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=qualified, and final.qualification.correct=true are authoritative for final qualification, read independently from study.final.*. Preserve this assessment during writing, review, audit, and revision; if later authoritative qualification differs, disclose failure and limitations rather than keeping this positive label. Qualification is neither a novelty certificate nor generalization evidence.\",\n    \"Private-only selection audit: study.selection_kind=baseline, study.investigation_outcome=completed_no_winner, study.search_score_delta=0, study.attempts.scored=3 and study.attempts.unscored=0. The frozen source equals the original baseline source. Keep search scores and final scores in their separate scopes. Failed alternatives have no grounded failure-cause diagnosis; do not choose between performance, validity, or adaptivity explanations without additional evaluator evidence.\",\n    \"Private-only final evidence audit: final.evidence.episodes is the quantitative source for every planned rule-specific comparison and recorded paired interval. It contains final seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019. Its candidate_diagnostics_trust is unverified. The source file checksum matches the manifest, and the final evaluator computes witness checks and small-panel audits, but do not promote any unverified candidate diagnostic or the exact_audit_pass field alone into a universal exactness claim. Attach explicit source slices and evaluator conventions to every derived result.\",\n    \"Private-only descriptive cohort check, derived from existing final records, not a new experiment: Figure 2 retains 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019. Median-minus-mean support-fraction averages are respectively -0.129630, -0.127451, -0.068627 at alpha=0.5 and +0.138889, +0.127451, +0.156863 at alpha=1.0. Recompute from selected support counts in the plotting script rather than hard-coding these rounded audit values. This subset analysis is descriptive and was chosen during framing.\",\n    \"Private-only metric semantics: final.metric.mean_effort_fraction uses a value of one for nonattainment, so it is a penalized summary, not the mean minimum investment of successful targets. final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction is relative to a verified lower bound and is not necessarily true optimizer error. Do not use these aggregates as substitutes for the paired cohort analysis or mix control cases into ordinary-result plots.\",\n    \"Uncertainty contract: final sampling seeds reuse fixed public panels and may share targets or model ancestry; they are not independent training replicates. Existing paired intervals use 200 cluster resamples with RNG seed 48271 and are conditional on fixed snapshots/rival pools. Keep all three seed summaries visible or tabulated. No existing cross-rule interval or p-value supports the reversal, and baseline intervals cannot be reassigned to it.\",\n    \"Main tables: a compact scope table distinguishing public source snapshots from the evaluated extracted panels; an investment/certificate table with cohort sizes and infeasible counts; and a matched policy-difference table at alpha=0.5 for both families and all three rules. Include ties with greedy; put the complete already-recorded interval grid after references. These are planned transformations of existing records, not unperformed experiments.\",\n    \"The main body must retain the exact six-task comparison, unconditional attainment rates, conditioning definitions, larger-panel interval caveat, uncertainty scope, and failed-search disclosure in brief. Supplementary material expands them; it must not hide contradictory or essential evidence. Route any separately supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses after references without inventing a ranking or retroactively changing this strategy.\",\n    \"Provenance contract: HELM v1.13.0; Open LLM contents revision 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2; separate results revision aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5. The final manifest records acquisition on 2026-11-17 and extracted records SHA-256 d80abb1b13c6962e15f04d9794fc18fcf39e91cf7c79c679e233d3031f934398. Use task/resources/final/manifest.json and records.json for source/normalization/license details. Do not claim evaluation of every row in the public repositories.\",\n    \"BBH scope: the existing manifest records model-revision reconciliation but explicitly says contents and results are distinct runs with potentially different settings. Auxiliary Open LLM odd slots route to BBH and even slots to the primary-family projection. Use the routing code to separate them, not the generic auxiliary label alone. BBH is not an independent replicated transfer benchmark.\",\n    \"Literature contract: cite only existing bibliography keys after safely reading the actual permitted source; retrieval metadata is not substantive reading. Many supplied entries are tangential, and several relevant starter records are absent, so bibliography curation and safe substantive reading remain writer-stage prerequisites before related-work claims. Do not fill space with unrelated citations or claim novelty against unread papers. Never search for the prohibited study, expand citation graphs, or infer its identity; log and stop any accidental exposure.\",\n    \"Figure production contract: only the overview uses generate-concept-figure, retaining editable source and prompt provenance. This stage plans figures and does not run that workflow. Quantitative figures must be script-generated vector PDF plus 300-DPI PNG; use the PDF in LaTeX whenever it exists. For a raster-only overview, regenerate at at least 2400 pixels wide at full width, or at least 1200 pixels if a later justified column-width placement is chosen; never upscale. Keep rendered labels at least 9pt and simplify rather than shrinking dense figures.\",\n    \"Keep artifact and internal decision terminology private. Reader-facing terms are target attainment rate, support fraction, strict first place, exact minimum support, certified infeasibility, and feasible support interval. Do not turn the selected reference into a novel learned method, or experimental controls into scientific success rates.\",\n    \"Required separate LLM Usage disclosure belongs after references if needed; remove names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, and identifying repository links. Describe only actual assistance documented in run artifacts. Do not claim human checks, literature reading, or training that did not occur.\",\n    \"This strategy creates neither a manuscript nor claim-mapping artifacts, generates no figures, performs no new research experiment, and does not run final-paper validation. A ready framing is not a claim that manuscript reproduction scripts, substantive citation verification, or a compiled paper already exist.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '44,130p' paper.tex; sed -n '130,218p' paper.tex; sed -n '390,493p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '44,130p' paper.tex; sed -n '130,218p' paper.tex; sed -n '390,493p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"\\paragraph{Validity beyond a published score.}\nBenchmark leakage and rephrased contamination motivate taking strategic score changes seriously \\citep{Cheater,Rephrased}, but do not establish our assumed response law. Behavioral testing \\citep{CheckList}, truthfulness evaluation \\citep{TruthfulQA}, replication on fresh test sets \\citep{ImageNetReplication}, and underspecification \\citep{Underspecification} each illustrate a distinct reason not to equate one aggregate with general capability. Dataset and model documentation support stating intended uses and limitations \\citep{Datasheets,ModelCards}. We follow that principle by distinguishing a hypothetical score intervention from observed training.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparisons and uncertainty.}\nComparisons across datasets require attention to the experimental unit \\citep{Demsar}, and selection itself can bias performance evaluation \\citep{Cawley}. Accordingly, our cross-rule reversal is descriptive and conditional on the observed snapshots and a post-selected framing. Recorded bootstrap intervals \\citep{Efron} concern paired selection policies, not the reversal hypothesis. The reproduction environment specifies NumPy and SciPy \\citep{NumPy,SciPy}; the summaries use NumPy arrays.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt]]\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and response model}\nLet $X\\in[0,1]^{n\\times m}$ be a public score matrix, with one row per evaluated model and one coordinate per task. Fix a target row $t$, its score vector $x=X_t$, and all other rows as rivals. For support $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and headroom fraction $\\alpha$, define\n\\begin{equation}\n x^{S,\\alpha}_j=x_j+\\alpha(1-x_j)\\,\\mathbf{1}\\{j\\in S\\}.\n \\label{eq:response}\n\\end{equation}\nAll selected coordinates receive the same fraction of remaining headroom. Unselected scores and all rival scores are unchanged. The evaluated ceilings are $\\alpha\\in\\{0.25,0.5,1\\}$. Full headroom allows a selected score to reach one; it is an optimistic stress condition, not an empirical prediction. Let $A_r$ denote aggregation rule $r$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n b_r=\\max_{i\\ne t} A_r(X_i),\\qquad\n \\cost_r(x,\\alpha)=\\min_S\\{|S|:A_r(x^{S,\\alpha})>b_r+\\eps\\},\n \\label{eq:cost}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\eps=10^{-10}$ implements strict first place. If no support wins, $\\cost_r=\\infty$. The normalized investment is $\\cost_r/m$ when attainable. A tie does not count as first place.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThis is an equal-coordinate cost model: intervening on a broad evaluation task and on a narrow subject each costs one within its own panel. Consequently, normalized support compares concentration within panels, not absolute work across datasets. It does not measure examples, money, accelerator time, or achievable learning gains. The assumptions exclude spillover, negative transfer, rivalry responses, and heterogeneity in the amount of work required to close headroom. Total score lift is available as a secondary diagnostic, but cannot calibrate those omitted quantities.\n\n\\subsection{Aggregation conditions and design rationale}\nFor sorted target values $z_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le z_{(m)}$, the mean is $m^{-1}\\sum_j z_j$. The conventional median takes the central order statistic for odd $m$ and averages the central pair for even $m$. The trimmed mean removes $q=\\lfloor m/5\\rfloor$ values from each end and averages the remaining $m-2q$. Tasks otherwise have equal weight. In the six-task panel this removes one value at each end, whereas on the subject panel trimming removes eleven at each end.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L70-L79]]\n\nThe research question is whether the relative minimum support for two rules changes with $\\alpha$. The mechanistic rationale is rank dependence: the mean rewards every positive uplift immediately; median and trimming rewards depend on where altered coordinates fall after sorting. Changing $\\alpha$ changes which ranks a coordinate can reach. It also changes each rule's attainable set. There is therefore no reason to interpret a difference computed only on currently successful targets as a pure cost difference without controlling the cohort. We assess response-dependent ordering directly, without attributing it to a new selection component.\n\nWithin any fixed rule and target, increasing $\\alpha$ cannot worsen attainability or increase the true minimum support: every coordinate and all three aggregators are monotone. This property does not constrain the sign of the difference between two rules' costs. A reversal of that difference is compatible with falling costs under both rules. Our primary contrast is the average median-minus-mean normalized support on targets attainable under both rules at both compared ceilings.\n\n\\subsection{Exact solutions, feasible witnesses, and bounds}\nThe frozen procedure begins by testing the full support. Since every allowed change is nonnegative and aggregation is coordinatewise monotone, failure of the full support certifies unattainability. This certificate is distinct from failure of a limited search. For a panel of at most six coordinates, supports are enumerated by cardinality, with deterministic semantic task-ID ordering. The first winning cardinality is exact. Thus every attainable primary six-task result used for the central contrast is a minimum-support result, not merely a successful heuristic plan.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L99-L117]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nFor the mean on larger panels, sort headrooms $h_j=1-x_j$ from largest to smallest. Writing $h_{[j]}$ for this ordering, the minimum is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n\\begin{equation}\n \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_j x_j+\\frac{\\alpha}{m}\\sum_{j=1}^k h_{[j]}>b_{\\mathrm{mean}}+\\eps.\n \\label{eq:mean}\n\\end{equation}\nNo size-$k$ set can have greater summed headroom than this prefix, so a shorter failing prefix excludes all shorter supports. This explains exactness without invoking an unobserved solver behavior. Ties are resolved by semantic task identity.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L118-L125]]\n\nFor larger median and trimmed panels, the procedure greedily adds the coordinate maximizing the immediate updated aggregate, again breaking ties by semantic task identity. It stops at a verified strict-first support. Its size is an upper bound $U$, not necessarily $\\cost_r$. Independently computed conservative lower bounds $L$ yield $L\\le\\cost_r\\le U$. The reported $L$ is the evaluator's verified reference bound, not a claim that the greedy procedure computes an exact optimum. Mean costs and exhaustive small-panel costs collapse to points. Unattainable cases have no finite support interval; the machine-readable out-of-range sentinel is never plotted as an investment cost.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126-L137]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L761-L789]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\n\\subsection{Public score panels and experimental protocol}\nThe primary panels are HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 with 57 subject coordinates and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates: IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro. The snapshots were acquired on 2026-11-17. The extracted records preserve original rows and settings; completeness and finite scores in the unit interval are required. These scores are not standardized effect sizes or observations from a homogeneous new evaluation. Source versions, checksums, filtering, and normalization are recorded in the supplementary reproduction specification.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/records.json]]\n\nModel aliases, normalized raw components, and score-map identities are grouped before partitioning the source records. The final evaluation uses a frozen, content-separated partition rather than a newly trained model. Within each family, rows tied for the lead under any studied rule are excluded from target selection. Remaining rows are sorted by mean score and divided into 24 bands; one target per band is sampled using each seed in $\\{1009,1013,1019\\}$. All rivals in the corresponding frozen pool remain visible. Each target is evaluated under every rule and ceiling, providing paired decisions rather than independently sampled rule-specific targets. Seeds vary target sampling, not model training.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L614-L687]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#seeds]]\n\nThe evaluator checks returned supports against the supplied matrix and accepts exactness only when independently supported. The ordinary primary cases define the reported target attainment rate: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim, divided by all primary targets in that cell. Constant-ceiling, zero-response, joint column-permutation, and reset-replay checks test specific consistency properties. Replay carries no statistical weight. The completed frozen evaluation satisfies its recorded qualification requirements; this does not certify generalization outside the snapshots or validate unverified auxiliary diagnostics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Comparators, conditioning, and uncertainty}\nMatched selection comparators are no intervention, a seeded random coordinate order, and the marginal-aggregate greedy policy. These are policies on the same target, panel, rule, and ceiling, not component ablations. For policy comparisons, define penalized effort $E=|S|/m$ for a verified successful support and $E=1$ otherwise. Assigning one to nonattainment is a reporting convention, not an observed minimum cost. Support plots instead condition on joint attainability and show genuine finite bounds.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L790-L818]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\nThe central comparison keeps a fixed cohort per seed across both rules and both ceilings. The three-rule support plots use the within-ceiling joint cohort, which may differ across ceilings; seed means receive equal weight. We show individual seed summaries rather than inventing a population confidence interval. Existing policy intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with resampling seed 48271 and percentile endpoints at 95\\%. They condition on the fixed snapshots and rival pools. They do not test differences between aggregation rules. Overlapping targets or related models across seeds further preclude treating these as independent training replications.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1046-L1068]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#paired_intervals]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Exact investment ordering changes with the response ceiling}\nOn the primary six-task panel, the fixed cohorts jointly attainable under mean and median at half and full headroom contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019, respectively. Figure~\\ref{fig:exact} shows the paired difference\n\\begin{equation}\n D_s(\\alpha)=\\frac{1}{|C_s|}\\sum_{t\\in C_s}\n \\frac{\\cost_{\\mathrm{median}}(X_t,\\alpha)-\\cost_{\\mathrm{mean}}(X_t,\\alpha)}{6}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe cohort $C_s$ is unchanged between panels. All included supports have exact minimum status backed by exhaustive selection. Therefore the comparison is about minimum investment in the specified model, not differences in heuristic success.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/exact_rule_contrast.pdf}\n\\caption{Exact paired contrast on fixed Open LLM cohorts. Negative values mean the median requires fewer modified coordinates on average. The same targets are retained at half and full headroom within each seed; cohort sizes are 18, 17, and 17. Points are descriptive seed summaries, not confidence intervals.}\n\\label{fig:exact}\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nAt half headroom, $D_s$ is $-0.1296$, $-0.1275$, and $-0.0686$ in seed order. At full headroom it is $+0.1389$, $+0.1275$, and $+0.1569$. Thus the average ordering reverses in every sampled cohort: median support is lower at half headroom and higher at full headroom. This does not mean each target reverses, nor does it estimate how frequently reversal would occur in a new leaderboard. The contrast was selected descriptively during analysis of the recorded results and is not a preregistered hypothesis test.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nHolding the cohort fixed is important. At half headroom, some targets cannot win under one or both rules. Comparing the mean on all mean-attainable targets with the median on all median-attainable targets would conflate ranking resistance with selection of different models. Our fixed-cohort comparison removes that particular change in composition, while remaining conditional on joint attainability. Its claim is consequently narrower, but also cleaner: response ceilings can change rule ordering even without changing the compared targets.\n\n\\subsection{Attainability and support answer different questions}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:scope} includes all ordinary primary targets in its attainment panels. Each rule--ceiling cell contains 72 target decisions across the sampling seeds. On Open LLM, quarter headroom yields attainment for 11 targets under the mean, one under the median, and eight under trimming. At half headroom these counts are 65, 52, and 67; full headroom attains all 72 under every rule. A median can therefore have lower average cost on the jointly attainable cohort while excluding more targets overall. Neither quantity should be substituted for the other.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#grid]]\n\nOn HELM, quarter-headroom counts are nine, nine, and eight for mean, median, and trimming; half-headroom counts are 42, 42, and 41. All rules attain all 72 targets at full headroom. The low-ceiling nonattainment here is certified by the all-coordinate test. It is not a count of unresolved heuristic failures. These snapshots support a strong role for response magnitude, but do not identify the response magnitudes that actual post-training would achieve.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#grid]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/attainability_and_support_scope.pdf}\n\\caption{Attainability and certificate scope. Left: all ordinary primary targets, with one strict-first definition throughout. Right: equal-seed average support bounds on targets attainable under all three rules at that ceiling. HELM cohort sizes are $(14,13,13)$ at half and $(24,24,24)$ at full headroom; Open LLM sizes are $(18,17,17)$ and $(24,24,24)$. Bars are certification ranges, not statistical intervals; points indicate exact costs. Unlike Figure~\\ref{fig:exact}, right-panel cohorts can change across ceilings.}\n\\label{fig:scope}\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#grid]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#support]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe right panels expose the difference between exact and bounded evidence. On the HELM joint cohort at half headroom, the exact mean support fraction is $0.483$, while median and trimmed support intervals are $[0.018,0.364]$ and $[0.205,0.414]$. These averaged intervals lie below the exact mean cost on that cohort. This establishes a lower average cost than the mean there even without resolving the individual order-statistic optima. At full headroom, the mean cost is $0.279$, while median and trimming intervals are $[0.018,0.330]$ and $[0.153,0.304]$. These overlap the mean point, so a reversal of the large-panel minimum-cost ordering is not established. A feasible upper bound above the mean does not prove a higher minimum.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#support]]\n\nOn the six-task joint cohorts the intervals collapse. Half-headroom mean, median, and trimming costs are $0.676$, $0.568$, and $0.568$; the full-headroom values are $0.382$, $0.484$, and $0.421$. These within-ceiling summaries give context for the matched result, but they use different cohorts at the two ceilings. The primary reversal claim rests on Figure~\\ref{fig:exact}, not on comparing these marginal averages.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#support]]\n\n\\subsection{Selection-policy comparisons do not imply optimizer novelty}\nTable~\\ref{tab:policies} compares matched policies at half headroom over all targets, using penalized effort. The frozen procedure ties marginal greedy for every HELM rule and for the six-task mean. This is expected from their shared selection behavior in these conditions. On the six-task median and trimmed mean, exhaustive selection attains smaller penalized effort than the recorded greedy policy. The distinction matters: marginal gain can fail to anticipate the benefit of a set of coordinates jointly changing an order statistic.\n% @evidence[[derived/comparators.tex]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L109-L137]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Matched policy comparison at half headroom. Lower penalized effort is better; nonattainment contributes one. Exact/witness denotes exhaustive selection on the six-task panel, sorted headroom for means, and a feasible greedy witness for larger order-statistic panels. Values average all targets with equal seed weights.}\n\\label{tab:policies}\n\\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}\n\\toprule\nPanel & Rule & Exact/witness & Greedy & Random & None\\\\\n\\midrule\n% BEGIN GENERATED comparators\nHELM & Mean & 0.707 & 0.707 & 0.835 & 1.000 \\\\\nHELM & Median & 0.639 & 0.639 & 0.780 & 1.000 \\\\\nHELM & Trimmed & 0.670 & 0.670 & 0.819 & 1.000 \\\\\nOpen LLM & Mean & 0.764 & 0.764 & 0.822 & 1.000 \\\\\nOpen LLM & Median & 0.688 & 0.799 & 0.831 & 1.000 \\\\\nOpen LLM & Trimmed & 0.650 & 0.688 & 0.762 & 1.000 \\\\\n% END GENERATED comparators\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/comparators.tex]]\n\\end{table}\n\nFor example, the six-task median has penalized effort $0.688$ versus greedy $0.799$ and random $0.831$; trimming has $0.650$, $0.688$, and $0.762$. No intervention yields one throughout, because target selection excludes existing leaders. These are observed policy comparisons under the response model, not improvements over a distinct evaluated architecture or a new exact algorithm. They also do not license claiming an average attainable minimum of $0.688$: that statistic includes unsuccessful targets by construction.\n% @evidence[[derived/comparators.tex]]\n\nRecorded cluster-resampling intervals for the policy contrasts, together with the complete rule--ceiling grid, appear in Supplementary Information. The intervals retain the unit of resampling used by the evaluator and are not reassigned to the cross-rule contrast. No controlled component ablation supports a mechanistic attribution to a proposed optimization refinement. The measured exact-versus-greedy difference is sufficient for the limited selection-policy conclusion.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#paired_intervals]]\n\n\\subsection{Scope checks and limits of the evaluated evidence}\nAuxiliary decisions include a six-coordinate HELM projection and a separately routed BBH panel with 24 recorded subtask coordinates. Open LLM auxiliary odd slots use BBH and even slots use the six-task family; those slots must not be interpreted as matched primary model identities. The BBH results are revision-matched but drawn from a distinct results snapshot, so matching model revisions does not establish identical evaluation settings. Their attainment and certificate counts are reported separately in the supplement, without pooling them into the primary findings.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L718-L732]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#reconciliation]]\n% @evidence[[derived/auxiliary.tex]]\n\nThe final evaluation is completed and qualified, with recorded control pass fraction $1.0$. Qualification concerns the evaluator's supported claims and specified consistency checks, not optimality of every large-panel witness or unbiasedness of the post-selected scientific framing. Candidate-supplied diagnostics remain unverified; our measurements use recorded evaluator decisions and inspectable source instead. There is no failed final qualification to reinterpret as a performance result. The absence of a qualifying alternative in development does not invalidate the completed frozen comparison, and neither fact establishes general superiority.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_pass_rate]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What investment means.}\nCounting coordinates isolates how aggregation rewards concentration, but it is not a training-production model. A unit of investment in a broad reasoning benchmark is not commensurate with a unit in an individual knowledge subject. Even within a panel, two tasks may have very different data access costs, learning curves, or contamination risks. Equal fractional headroom is useful because it is transparent, monotone, and bounded by the score range. It is not justified as a fitted account of any particular training process. Conclusions should therefore be stated conditionally: \\emph{if} these coordinate responses are available, this support suffices or is necessary.\n\n\\paragraph{What the reversal does and does not show.}\nThe result rules out treating the observed mean--median ordering as independent of the permitted response ceiling in this setting. It does not establish a universal ordering, a population reversal frequency, or a theorem that medians are generally easy to manipulate. Changing the rival pool changes the threshold; changing the target distribution changes which costs are averaged. The fixed-cohort design controls one source of composition bias, but conditions away targets that fail under either rule at half headroom. Attainment plots are essential precisely because those excluded targets are substantively important. Likewise, the three-rule support cohorts serve a different question from the two-rule fixed cohorts and cannot be silently interchanged.\n\n\\paragraph{Data and inference limits.}\nSnapshots include heterogeneous model settings and related model families. Content-based partitioning reduces literal overlap but does not ensure independence of model ancestry, shared training data, or benchmark familiarity. Seeds sample frozen rows rather than generate new learning outcomes. The framing was chosen after inspecting the recorded decisions; an independent snapshot or preregistered comparison would be needed for stronger confirmatory claims. Existing policy bootstrap intervals quantify only their conditional resampling variability. They omit uncertainty in benchmark scores, data acquisition, response calibration, rival selection, and the choice of framing.\n\n\\paragraph{Certificates and practical use.}\n1013 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 0.50 & Random & -0.097 & [-0.139, -0.062] \\\\\n1013 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 0.50 & Greedy & -0.035 & [-0.063, -0.007] \\\\\n1013 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 1.00 & Random & -0.021 & [-0.049, +0.000] \\\\\n1013 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 1.00 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Mean & 0.25 & Random & -0.042 & [-0.080, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Mean & 0.25 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Mean & 0.50 & Random & -0.135 & [-0.188, -0.089] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Mean & 0.50 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Mean & 1.00 & Random & -0.193 & [-0.214, -0.175] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Mean & 1.00 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Median & 0.25 & Random & -0.019 & [-0.057, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Median & 0.25 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Median & 0.50 & Random & -0.154 & [-0.235, -0.083] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Median & 0.50 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Median & 1.00 & Random & -0.045 & [-0.062, -0.028] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Median & 1.00 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Trimmed & 0.25 & Random & -0.042 & [-0.099, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Trimmed & 0.25 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Trimmed & 0.50 & Random & -0.147 & [-0.203, -0.091] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Trimmed & 0.50 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Trimmed & 1.00 & Random & -0.080 & [-0.093, -0.064] \\\\\n1019 & HELM & Trimmed & 1.00 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Mean & 0.25 & Random & -0.014 & [-0.035, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Mean & 0.25 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Mean & 0.50 & Random & -0.069 & [-0.104, -0.041] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Mean & 0.50 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Mean & 1.00 & Random & -0.104 & [-0.132, -0.076] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Mean & 1.00 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Median & 0.25 & Random & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Median & 0.25 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Median & 0.50 & Random & -0.104 & [-0.167, -0.056] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Median & 0.50 & Greedy & -0.097 & [-0.139, -0.063] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Median & 1.00 & Random & -0.021 & [-0.049, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Median & 1.00 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 0.25 & Random & -0.021 & [-0.056, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 0.25 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 0.50 & Random & -0.097 & [-0.139, -0.069] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 0.50 & Greedy & -0.028 & [-0.056, -0.007] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 1.00 & Random & -0.042 & [-0.069, -0.021] \\\\\n1019 & Open LLM & Trimmed & 1.00 & Greedy & +0.000 & [+0.000, +0.000] \\\\\n% END GENERATED paired_intervals\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{longtable}\n% @evidence[[derived/paired_intervals.tex]]\n\n\\subsection{Auxiliary projections and distinct BBH sensitivity}\nThe HELM auxiliary projection uses six semantic task IDs, while BBH uses its own recorded subtask panel. Open LLM auxiliary slots alternate the primary-family scores with BBH according to slot parity. The table below keeps all three auxiliary scopes separate. Counts pool recorded decisions across seeds but do not assert independent model identities or equal settings. Bounded counts identify successful supports whose exact minimum is unresolved. They should not be read as implementation failures or inferred minimum costs.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L718-L732]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#auxiliary]]\n\\begin{longtable}{llllr}\n\\caption{Auxiliary attainment and bounded-support counts, separate from primary results.}\\\\\n\\toprule Scope & Rule & $\\alpha$ & Attained & Bounded\\\\\\midrule\n\\endfirsthead\n\\toprule Scope & Rule & $\\alpha$ & Attained & Bounded\\\\\\midrule\n\\endhead\n% BEGIN GENERATED auxiliary\nHELM projection & Mean & 0.25 & 6/72 & 0 \\\\\nHELM projection & Mean & 0.50 & 31/72 & 0 \\\\\nHELM projection & Mean & 1.00 & 72/72 & 0 \\\\\nHELM projection & Median & 0.25 & 3/72 & 0 \\\\\nHELM projection & Median & 0.50 & 34/72 & 0 \\\\\nHELM projection & Median & 1.00 & 72/72 & 0 \\\\\nHELM projection & Trimmed & 0.25 & 3/72 & 0 \\\\\nHELM projection & Trimmed & 0.50 & 26/72 & 0 \\\\\nHELM projection & Trimmed & 1.00 & 72/72 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Mean & 0.25 & 5/36 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Mean & 0.50 & 32/36 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Mean & 1.00 & 36/36 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Median & 0.25 & 0/36 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Median & 0.50 & 25/36 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Median & 1.00 & 36/36 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Trimmed & 0.25 & 3/36 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Trimmed & 0.50 & 33/36 & 0 \\\\\nOpen LLM six & Trimmed & 1.00 & 36/36 & 0 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Mean & 0.25 & 8/36 & 0 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Mean & 0.50 & 30/36 & 0 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Mean & 1.00 & 36/36 & 0 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Median & 0.25 & 8/36 & 8 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Median & 0.50 & 27/36 & 27 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Median & 1.00 & 36/36 & 36 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Trimmed & 0.25 & 8/36 & 8 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Trimmed & 0.50 & 29/36 & 29 \\\\\nBBH 24 & Trimmed & 1.00 & 36/36 & 36 \\\\\n% END GENERATED auxiliary\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{longtable}\n% @evidence[[derived/auxiliary.tex]]\n\n\\subsection{Consistency checks, selection disclosure, and reporting limits}\nConstant-ceiling controls provide no strict winner. Zero-response controls cannot improve a nonleader. Joint nonidentity column permutations test semantic-coordinate consistency, and reset replay checks repeatability of logical outputs. Recorded control pass fraction is $1.0$ and final qualification is completed and qualified. These checks are narrow behavioral checks; they do not certify real training effects, independent datasets, absence of every adaptive choice, or exactness of large-panel order-statistic solutions. The diagnostic trust field remains unverified and supplies no extra empirical claim.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_pass_rate]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n\nDevelopment selection retained the reference procedure: three alternatives were scored, none unscored, no alternative met the recorded selection requirements, and the retained development-score change was zero. Those development outcomes are distinct from the independently completed final evaluation. The available feedback does not identify why the alternatives failed qualification; neither statistical underperformance nor a validity/adaptivity defect is asserted as the sole cause. No missing performance evidence is counted as a measured loss. These unsuccessful comparisons do not constitute controlled component ablations.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study]]\n\nThe mean verified gap in the evaluator measures separation from its verified lower bound, not necessarily distance from the true optimum. Its aggregate penalized effort assigns one to nonattainment. Neither aggregate is substituted for the cohort-specific exact costs or intervals reported here. Publication suitability and the existence of a compiled manuscript do not alter the scientific qualification of any evaluated condition.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLanguage-model assistance was used to formulate candidate research directions, prepare solver code, plan reference queries, select the conditional framing, inspect recorded evidence, derive manuscript summaries, author plotting and reproduction scripts, and draft and revise this manuscript. Reference use is limited to inspected canonical metadata and abstracts; no citation graph was expanded. The overview figure is assigned to an automated concept-figure workflow. No model was trained or evaluated for this paper-writing step, and no new research evaluation was run. Automated consistency and compilation checks are described as artifact checks, not as an independent scientific review.\n\n\\end{document}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '33,56p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '79,136p'; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '33,56p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '79,136p'; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"    33\t\\caption{The studied counterfactual: modify only selected target coordinates, hold rivals fixed, and compare aggregation conditions. The outcome is an exact minimum support, certified unattainability, or a lower--upper support interval. No model training is represented.}\n    34\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    35\t\\end{figure}\n    36\t\n    37\t\\section{Related Work}\n    38\t\\paragraph{Evaluation breadth and benchmark composition.}\n    39\tMMLU evaluates knowledge across subjects \\citep{MMLU}; HELM emphasizes broad, transparent evaluation across scenarios and metrics \\citep{HELM}. BIG-bench and BBH characterize diverse tasks and difficult reasoning settings \\citep{BIGBench,BBH}. The six-task panel studied here combines instruction following \\citep{IFEval}, challenging mathematics \\citep{MATH}, expert question answering \\citep{GPQA}, narrative reasoning \\citep{MuSR}, and knowledge-oriented reasoning \\citep{MMLUPro}, alongside BBH. These source works explain the tasks' purposes; our empirical claims concern the pinned score snapshots, not a replication of their original experiments. The benchmark-lottery perspective emphasizes that relative performance depends on benchmark choices \\citep{Lottery}. We instead hold task membership fixed in the principal comparison and vary the aggregation rule and allowed target response.\n    40\t\n    41\t\\paragraph{Aggregation and statistical robustness.}\n    42\t\\citet{SocialChoice} interpret tasks as voters and evaluation as social aggregation. Our comparison uses cardinal score summaries rather than implementing their ordinal voting framework. Classical robust estimation studies sensitivity to contamination of observations \\citep{Huber}. Resistance of a location estimate to contamination is not the same as support needed to exceed a rival's aggregation threshold under bounded upward changes. In particular, the target's order statistics and the rival threshold both matter here. We make no claim that the familiar mean, median, trimmed mean, exhaustive enumeration, or greedy policies are new.\n    43\t\n    44\t\\paragraph{Validity beyond a published score.}\n    45\tBenchmark leakage and rephrased contamination motivate taking strategic score changes seriously \\citep{Cheater,Rephrased}, but do not establish our assumed response law. Behavioral testing \\citep{CheckList}, truthfulness evaluation \\citep{TruthfulQA}, replication on fresh test sets \\citep{ImageNetReplication}, and underspecification \\citep{Underspecification} each illustrate a distinct reason not to equate one aggregate with general capability. Dataset and model documentation support stating intended uses and limitations \\citep{Datasheets,ModelCards}. We follow that principle by distinguishing a hypothetical score intervention from observed training.\n    46\t\n    47\t\\paragraph{Comparisons and uncertainty.}\n    48\tComparisons across datasets require attention to the experimental unit \\citep{Demsar}, and selection itself can bias performance evaluation \\citep{Cawley}. Accordingly, our cross-rule reversal is descriptive and conditional on the observed snapshots and a post-selected framing. Recorded bootstrap intervals \\citep{Efron} concern paired selection policies, not the reversal hypothesis. The reproduction environment specifies NumPy and SciPy \\citep{NumPy,SciPy}; the summaries use NumPy arrays.\n    49\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt]]\n    50\t\n    51\t\\section{Method}\n    52\t\\subsection{Problem setup and response model}\n    53\tLet $X\\in[0,1]^{n\\times m}$ be a public score matrix, with one row per evaluated model and one coordinate per task. Fix a target row $t$, its score vector $x=X_t$, and all other rows as rivals. For support $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and headroom fraction $\\alpha$, define\n    54\t\\begin{equation}\n    55\t x^{S,\\alpha}_j=x_j+\\alpha(1-x_j)\\,\\mathbf{1}\\{j\\in S\\}.\n    56\t \\label{eq:response}\n    79\tThe frozen procedure begins by testing the full support. Since every allowed change is nonnegative and aggregation is coordinatewise monotone, failure of the full support certifies unattainability. This certificate is distinct from failure of a limited search. For a panel of at most six coordinates, supports are enumerated by cardinality, with deterministic semantic task-ID ordering. The first winning cardinality is exact. Thus every attainable primary six-task result used for the central contrast is a minimum-support result, not merely a successful heuristic plan.\n    80\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L99-L117]]\n    81\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n    82\t\n    83\tFor the mean on larger panels, sort headrooms $h_j=1-x_j$ from largest to smallest. Writing $h_{[j]}$ for this ordering, the minimum is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n    84\t\\begin{equation}\n    85\t \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_j x_j+\\frac{\\alpha}{m}\\sum_{j=1}^k h_{[j]}>b_{\\mathrm{mean}}+\\eps.\n    86\t \\label{eq:mean}\n    87\t\\end{equation}\n    88\tNo size-$k$ set can have greater summed headroom than this prefix, so a shorter failing prefix excludes all shorter supports. This explains exactness without invoking an unobserved solver behavior. Ties are resolved by semantic task identity.\n    89\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L118-L125]]\n    90\t\n    91\tFor larger median and trimmed panels, the procedure greedily adds the coordinate maximizing the immediate updated aggregate, again breaking ties by semantic task identity. It stops at a verified strict-first support. Its size is an upper bound $U$, not necessarily $\\cost_r$. Independently computed conservative lower bounds $L$ yield $L\\le\\cost_r\\le U$. The reported $L$ is the evaluator's verified reference bound, not a claim that the greedy procedure computes an exact optimum. Mean costs and exhaustive small-panel costs collapse to points. Unattainable cases have no finite support interval; the machine-readable out-of-range sentinel is never plotted as an investment cost.\n    92\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126-L137]]\n    93\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L761-L789]]\n    94\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n    95\t\n    96\t\\subsection{Public score panels and experimental protocol}\n    97\tThe primary panels are HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 with 57 subject coordinates and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates: IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro. The snapshots were acquired on 2026-11-17. The extracted records preserve original rows and settings; completeness and finite scores in the unit interval are required. These scores are not standardized effect sizes or observations from a homogeneous new evaluation. Source versions, checksums, filtering, and normalization are recorded in the supplementary reproduction specification.\n    98\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json]]\n    99\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/records.json]]\n   100\t\n   101\tModel aliases, normalized raw components, and score-map identities are grouped before partitioning the source records. The final evaluation uses a frozen, content-separated partition rather than a newly trained model. Within each family, rows tied for the lead under any studied rule are excluded from target selection. Remaining rows are sorted by mean score and divided into 24 bands; one target per band is sampled using each seed in $\\{1009,1013,1019\\}$. All rivals in the corresponding frozen pool remain visible. Each target is evaluated under every rule and ceiling, providing paired decisions rather than independently sampled rule-specific targets. Seeds vary target sampling, not model training.\n   102\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L614-L687]]\n   103\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#seeds]]\n   104\t\n   105\tThe evaluator checks returned supports against the supplied matrix and accepts exactness only when independently supported. The ordinary primary cases define the reported target attainment rate: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim, divided by all primary targets in that cell. Constant-ceiling, zero-response, joint column-permutation, and reset-replay checks test specific consistency properties. Replay carries no statistical weight. The completed frozen evaluation satisfies its recorded qualification requirements; this does not certify generalization outside the snapshots or validate unverified auxiliary diagnostics.\n   106\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n   107\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n   108\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n   109\t\n   110\t\\subsection{Comparators, conditioning, and uncertainty}\n   111\tMatched selection comparators are no intervention, a seeded random coordinate order, and the marginal-aggregate greedy policy. These are policies on the same target, panel, rule, and ceiling, not component ablations. For policy comparisons, define penalized effort $E=|S|/m$ for a verified successful support and $E=1$ otherwise. Assigning one to nonattainment is a reporting convention, not an observed minimum cost. Support plots instead condition on joint attainability and show genuine finite bounds.\n   112\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L790-L818]]\n   113\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n   114\t\n   115\tThe central comparison keeps a fixed cohort per seed across both rules and both ceilings. The three-rule support plots use the within-ceiling joint cohort, which may differ across ceilings; seed means receive equal weight. We show individual seed summaries rather than inventing a population confidence interval. Existing policy intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with resampling seed 48271 and percentile endpoints at 95\\%. They condition on the fixed snapshots and rival pools. They do not test differences between aggregation rules. Overlapping targets or related models across seeds further preclude treating these as independent training replications.\n   116\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1046-L1068]]\n   117\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#paired_intervals]]\n   118\t\n   119\t\\section{Results}\n   120\t\\subsection{Exact investment ordering changes with the response ceiling}\n   121\tOn the primary six-task panel, the fixed cohorts jointly attainable under mean and median at half and full headroom contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019, respectively. Figure~\\ref{fig:exact} shows the paired difference\n   122\t\\begin{equation}\n   123\t D_s(\\alpha)=\\frac{1}{|C_s|}\\sum_{t\\in C_s}\n   124\t \\frac{\\cost_{\\mathrm{median}}(X_t,\\alpha)-\\cost_{\\mathrm{mean}}(X_t,\\alpha)}{6}.\n   125\t\\end{equation}\n   126\tThe cohort $C_s$ is unchanged between panels. All included supports have exact minimum status backed by exhaustive selection. Therefore the comparison is about minimum investment in the specified model, not differences in heuristic success.\n   127\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n   128\t\n   129\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   130\t\\centering\n   131\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/exact_rule_contrast.pdf}\n   132\t\\caption{Exact paired contrast on fixed Open LLM cohorts. Negative values mean the median requires fewer modified coordinates on average. The same targets are retained at half and full headroom within each seed; cohort sizes are 18, 17, and 17. Points are descriptive seed summaries, not confidence intervals.}\n   133\t\\label{fig:exact}\n   134\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n   135\t\\end{figure}\n   136\t\n@article{MMLU,\n title={{Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding}},\n author={Hendrycks, Dan and Burns, Collin and Basart, Steven and Zou, Andy and Mazeika, Mantas and Song, Dawn and Steinhardt, Jacob},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2009.03300},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03300}\n}\n\n@article{BBH,\n title={{Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them}},\n author={Suzgun, Mirac and Scales, Nathan and Schärli, Nathanael and Gehrmann, Sebastian and Tay, Yi and Chung, Hyung Won and others},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.09261},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09261}\n}\n\n@article{HELM,\n title={{Holistic Evaluation of Language Models}},\n author={Liang, Percy and Bommasani, Rishi and Lee, Tony and Tsipras, Dimitris and Soylu, Dilara and Yasunaga, Michihiro and others},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2211.09110},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09110}\n}\n\n@article{Lottery,\n title={{The Benchmark Lottery}},\n author={Dehghani, Mostafa and Tay, Yi and Gritsenko, Alexey A. and Zhao, Zhe and Houlsby, Neil and Diaz, Fernando and Metzler, Donald and Vinyals, Oriol},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.07002},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07002}\n}\n\n@article{SocialChoice,\n title={{Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory}},\n author={Lanctot, Marc and Larson, Kate and Bachrach, Yoram and Marris, Luke and Li, Zun and Bhoopchand, Avishkar and others},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2312.03121},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03121}\n}\n\n@article{Cheater,\n title={{Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater}},\n author={Zhou, Kun and Zhu, Yutao and Chen, Zhipeng and Chen, Wentong and Zhao, Wayne Xin and Chen, Xu and others},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.01964},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01964}\n}\n\n@article{Rephrased,\n title={{Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples}},\n author={Yang, Shuo and Chiang, Wei-Lin and Zheng, Lianmin and Gonzalez, Joseph E. and Stoica, Ion},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.04850}\n}\n\n@article{GPQA,\n title={{GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q\\&A Benchmark}},\n author={Rein, David and Hou, Betty Li and Stickland, Asa Cooper and Petty, Jackson and Pang, Richard Yuanzhe and Dirani, Julien and Michael, Julian and Bowman, Samuel R.},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.12022},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12022}\n}\n\n@article{IFEval,\n title={{Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models}},\n author={Zhou, Jeffrey and Lu, Tianjian and Mishra, Swaroop and Brahma, Siddhartha and Basu, Sujoy and Luan, Yi and Zhou, Denny and Hou, Le},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.07911},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.07911}\n}\n\n@article{MMLUPro,\n title={{MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark}},\n author={Wang, Yubo and Ma, Xueguang and Zhang, Ge and Ni, Yuansheng and Chandra, Abhranil and Guo, Shiguang and others},\n year={2024},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.01574},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01574}\n}\n\n@article{MATH,\n title={{Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset}},\n author={Hendrycks, Dan and Burns, Collin and Kadavath, Saurav and Arora, Akul and Basart, Steven and Tang, Eric and Song, Dawn and Steinhardt, Jacob},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2103.03874},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.03874}\n}\n\n@article{MuSR,\n title={{MuSR: Testing the Limits of Chain-of-thought with Multistep Soft Reasoning}},\n author={Sprague, Zayne and Ye, Xi and Bostrom, Kaj and Chaudhuri, Swarat and Durrett, Greg},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.16049},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.16049}\n}\n\n@article{BIGBench,\n title={{Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models}},\n author={Srivastava, Aarohi and Rastogi, Abhinav and Rao, Abhishek and Shoeb, Abu Awal Md and Abid, Abubakar and Fisch, Adam and others},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2206.04615},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.04615}\n}\n\n@article{Datasheets,\n title={{Datasheets for Datasets}},\n author={Gebru, Timnit and Morgenstern, Jamie and Vecchione, Briana and Vaughan, Jennifer Wortman and Wallach, Hanna and Daumé III, Hal and Crawford, Kate},\n year={2018},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1803.09010},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010}\n}\n\n@article{ModelCards,\n title={{Model Cards for Model Reporting}},\n author={Mitchell, Margaret and Wu, Simone and Zaldivar, Andrew and Barnes, Parker and Vasserman, Lucy and Hutchinson, Ben and others},\n year={2018},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1810.03993},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993}\n}\n\n@article{CheckList,\n title={{Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP models with CheckList}},\n author={Ribeiro, Marco Tulio and Wu, Tongshuang and Guestrin, Carlos and Singh, Sameer},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2005.04118},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.04118}\n}\n\n@article{Underspecification,\n title={{Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning}},\n author={D'Amour, Alexander and Heller, Katherine and Moldovan, Dan and Adlam, Ben and Alipanahi, Babak and Beutel, Alex and others},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2011.03395},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.03395}\n}\n\n@article{ImageNetReplication,\n title={{Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?}},\n author={Recht, Benjamin and Roelofs, Rebecca and Schmidt, Ludwig and Shankar, Vaishaal},\n year={2019},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1902.10811},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10811}\n}\n\n@article{TruthfulQA,\n title={{TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods}},\n author={Lin, Stephanie and Hilton, Jacob and Evans, Owain},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2109.07958},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07958}\n}\n\n@article{NumPy,\n title={{Array Programming with NumPy}},\n author={Harris, Charles R. and Millman, K. Jarrod and van der Walt, Stéfan J. and Gommers, Ralf and Virtanen, Pauli and Cournapeau, David and others},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.10256},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.10256}\n}\n\n@article{SciPy,\n title={{SciPy 1.0--Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python}},\n author={Virtanen, Pauli and Gommers, Ralf and Oliphant, Travis E. and Haberland, Matt and Reddy, Tyler and Cournapeau, David and others},\n year={2019},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1907.10121},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.10121}\n}\n\n@article{Demsar,\n title={{Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets}},\n author={Janez DemÅ¡ar},\n year={2006},\n journal={Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n url={https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v7/demsar06a.html}\n}\n\n@article{Cawley,\n title={{On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation}},\n author={Gavin C. Cawley and Nicola L. C. Talbot},\n year={2010},\n journal={Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n url={https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html}\n}\n\n@article{Huber,\n title={{Robust Estimation of a Location Parameter}},\n author={Peter J. Huber},\n year={1964},\n journal={The Annals of Mathematical Statistics},\n doi={10.1214/aoms/1177703732}\n}\n\n@article{Efron,\n title={{Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife}},\n author={B. Efron},\n year={1979},\n journal={The Annals of Statistics},\n doi={10.1214/aos/1176344552}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
