{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"jq -r '.claims[] | [.claim, (.citation_keys|join(\\\",\\\"))] | @tsv' claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"The studied response modifies selected target coordinates by a common fraction of remaining headroom, fixes rivals, and counts support rather than training cost.\t\nEvaluated headroom fractions are one quarter, one half, and full headroom; strict first place requires a margin exceeding 1e-10.\t\nThe median averages the central pair on even panels; symmetric trimming removes floor(m/5) values from each tail.\t\nThe primary panels have 57 HELM subjects and six Open LLM task coordinates; the separate BBH sensitivity uses 24 recorded subtasks.\tMMLU,HELM,BBH\nThe public score inputs are the pinned HELM release, contents revision, and distinct results revision acquired on the recorded date, with the reported extracted-record checksum.\t\nThe protocol samples one nonleading target in each of 24 score strata using seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019, paired across aggregation rules and ceilings.\t\nFull-support failure certifies unattainability for these monotone aggregators; it is not merely a limited-search failure.\t\nThe selected implementation enumerates supports up to six coordinates, solves larger means exactly by sorted headroom, and uses marginal greedy witnesses for larger order-statistic panels.\t\nLower bounds are independently verified reference bounds; feasible large-panel order-statistic supports are upper bounds, not exact costs.\t\nThe fixed jointly attainable mean-median cohorts contain 18, 17, and 17 targets, and every cost included in their contrast is exact.\t\nIn each fixed six-task cohort the average median-minus-mean support fraction is negative at half headroom and positive at full headroom; all reported values and abstract ranges are derived from the same exact contrasts.\t\nThe primary grid contains 72 target decisions per rule-ceiling cell, not necessarily independent unique models.\t\nOpen LLM attainment counts at quarter, half, and full headroom differ across mean, median, and trimming as reported in the main text and complete grid.\t\nHELM attainment counts at quarter, half, and full headroom are those in the main text and complete grid; all ordinary primary nonattainment is certified infeasible.\t\nAll primary targets attain first place at full headroom in the recorded panels.\t\nFigure 3 certification intervals use equal seed weights and within-ceiling three-rule joint cohorts; the caption reports all cohort sizes.\t\nHELM half-headroom order-statistic average upper bounds lie below the exact mean cost on the joint cohort, while full-headroom intervals overlap the mean and do not establish reversal of minimum costs.\t\nThe reported six-task three-rule support averages are exact within their declared jointly attainable ceiling-specific cohorts, which differ across ceilings.\t\nThe main half-headroom table reports all-target penalized effort for exact/witness, marginal greedy, random, and no-intervention policies; nonattainment contributes one.\t\nThe frozen procedure ties greedy on HELM and the six-task mean, and has smaller penalized effort on the six-task median and trimmed mean at half headroom.\t\nEvery scalar in the complete primary table is reproduced from the ordinary primary evaluator decisions, with success and penalty semantics kept separate.\t\nRecorded conditional paired policy intervals use 200 cluster bootstrap resamples, RNG seed 48271, and 95-percent percentile endpoints; these do not test cross-rule ordering.\tEfron\nEvery row of the supplementary policy interval table reproduces the recorded family, rule, ceiling, seed, comparator, difference, and interval endpoints.\t\nAuxiliary HELM projections, Open LLM even slots, and BBH odd slots have separately reported counts; BBH revision matching does not establish identical evaluation settings.\t\nThe completed final evaluation is qualified; this does not imply unrestricted validity or exact large-panel order-statistic costs.\t\nThe recorded final control pass fraction is one; candidate diagnostics remain unverified.\t\nThe retained selection is the reference baseline, with no qualifying development winner and zero retained development-score change.\t\nThree development alternatives were scored and none unscored; their qualification-failure causes are not identified, and they are not controlled component ablations.\t\nThe reported source locations support the exact evaluated implementation, whose selected and baseline source copies match.\t\nThe final evaluator uses CPU computation, its documented wall and method reservations and advisory decision budget; dependency versions are the supplied versions.\t\nRaw-score documentation, normalization, content partitioning, and uncertain score-data redistribution rights are as recorded by the acquisition manifest.\t\nThe study does not measure training responses, practical expenditure, causal contamination effects, or population superiority; the observed ordering is conditional and descriptively selected.\t\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding.\tMMLU\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them.\tBBH\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Holistic Evaluation of Language Models.\tHELM\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of The Benchmark Lottery.\tLottery\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory.\tSocialChoice\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater.\tCheater\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples.\tRephrased\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q&A Benchmark.\tGPQA\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models.\tIFEval\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark.\tMMLUPro\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset.\tMATH\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of MuSR: Testing the Limits of Chain-of-thought with Multistep Soft Reasoning.\tMuSR\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models.\tBIGBench\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Datasheets for Datasets.\tDatasheets\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Model Cards for Model Reporting.\tModelCards\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP models with CheckList.\tCheckList\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning.\tUnderspecification\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?.\tImageNetReplication\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods.\tTruthfulQA\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Array Programming with NumPy.\tNumPy\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of SciPy 1.0--Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python.\tSciPy\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets.\tDemsar\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation.\tCawley\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Robust Estimation of a Location Parameter.\tHuber\nLiterature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife.\tEfron\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
